UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 26, Lesson 1: What is an ecosystem', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nExplain one living-systems idea for what is an ecosystem with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.\n\nWATCH FOR\nMixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a task on what is an ecosystem: produce notes/diagrams/data as appropriate, include evidence-linked claims, follow safety rules, and write a five-sentence reflection with one next science skill goal.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science","learning_intention":"Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["SC4-LW-04"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a task on what is an ecosystem: produce notes/diagrams/data as appropriate, include evidence-linked claims, follow safety rules, and write a five-sentence reflection with one next science skill goa Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about what is an ecosystem linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.. Correction: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses what is an ecosystem. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about what is an ecosystem, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about what is an ecosystem. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for what is an ecosystem. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for what is an ecosystem. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Explain one living-systems idea for what is an ecosystem with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for what is an ecosystem; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based","accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link","Careful use of evidence and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with what is an ecosystem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named evidence and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply what is an ecosystem to new material and name the evidence and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way","Name evidence and explain why it matters only, without linking it to what is an ecosystem"],"question":"Which response best applies what is an ecosystem with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way"],"question":"New examples: steam from a kettle, a germinating bean, a plastic dinosaur, a granite pebble. Which claim about what is an ecosystem is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way","A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation","A label copied from the title with no evidence"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about what is an ecosystem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about what is an ecosystem with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way"],"question":"Using this science idea — Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Identify the independent variable, control the rest, and record repeatable measurements","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about what is an ecosystem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded","If the observed feature changes, the claim about what is an ecosystem should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true"],"question":"Which prediction about what is an ecosystem can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about what is an ecosystem. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses what is an ecosystem. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about what is an ecosystem linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for what is an ecosystem. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about what is an ecosystem, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.. Correction: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-sci-y7-w26-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise what is an ecosystem.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Living World","success_criteria":["I can use what is an ecosystem with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify evidence and explain why it matters.","I can practise safety independently on a new prompt.","I can notice model needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about what is an ecosystem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for What is an ecosystem: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake) or place an organism in a food web with arrows showing energy flow.\n\nTRY FIRST\nExplain one living-systems idea for what is an ecosystem with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","explain":"Year 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"What is an ecosystem.","caption":"Link claim to evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">science concept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">data/obs</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example for What is an ecosystem: relate structure to function (e.g