e observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Science habits.","caption":"Models help explain.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Safety</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">school-safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">next skill</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about what is an ecosystem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Here is a more productive model: Example for What is an ecosystem: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake) or place an organism in a food web with arrows showing energy flow.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about what is an ecosystem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nYear 7 science develops what is an ecosystem using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record 